var tafsirData_48 = {
  "1": "“Indeed, We have granted you a clear triumph O Prophet” opens with emphatic certainty. The triumph is presented as already granted even though its full effects would unfold afterward. It refers first to the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, when Muhammad and his Companions were prevented from entering Mecca and agreed to terms some believers initially found difficult.\n\nAllah named that apparent setback a “clear triumph.” The truce reduced warfare, recognized the Muslim community as a negotiating power, opened safer contact for people to hear Islam, and prepared later successes. The Muslims would return for pilgrimage, and many entered faith during the period of peace. Victory was deeper than immediate battlefield gain.\n\n“We have granted you” attributes the opening entirely to Allah. Prophetic judgment, patient negotiation, and Companion obedience were means, but the result was His gift. The word translated triumph also carries the sense of opening: closed paths were opened by a settlement that short-term emotion struggled to understand.\n\nThe verse teaches believers not to measure success only by visible dominance. A principled compromise that protects the mission may achieve what confrontation cannot. This does not make every unfavorable agreement a victory; Hudaybiyyah was guided by Allah and kept lawful essentials intact. It does teach patience with outcomes whose wisdom is still unfolding. Clear triumph may arrive wearing the appearance of delay, provided obedience and trust guide the path.\n\nHudaybiyyah also shows the importance of Prophetic leadership when sincere followers cannot yet see the wisdom. Their questions and pain were real, but they ultimately obeyed. Later clarity did not mean their earlier feelings were faithless; it showed that revelation sees farther than immediate experience. The verse’s certainty offered a new lens before all evidence appeared. In personal life, believers should not label every disappointment a hidden victory, since they lack revelation about outcomes. They can, however, refuse despair, maintain lawful principle, consult wisely, and leave room for Allah to open benefit through an unwelcome turn.",
  "2": "“So that Allah may forgive you for your past and future shortcomings” connects the clear triumph with special honor for Muhammad. The project wording says “shortcomings,” not a life of deliberate rebellion. Prophets are protected in conveying revelation and are exemplary servants; their continual forgiveness reflects humility, human limitation, and Allah raising them to ever higher completion.\n\nScholars explained the comprehensive phrase as a declaration of complete divine pardon and security for the Prophet. It should increase love and respect for him, not invite the invention of sins that revelation does not name. Muhammad still sought forgiveness frequently, teaching gratitude rather than complacency.\n\n“Perfect His favour upon you” includes completion of support, establishment of the religion, and the unfolding blessings opened by Hudaybiyyah. Favor is perfected according to Allah’s wise plan, not necessarily through immediate ease.\n\n“Guide you along the Straight Path” does not imply that the Prophet was previously astray from faith. It means continued guidance, firmness, and leading along the most direct course in each new stage. Even the best-guided servant receives further guidance from Allah.\n\nThe verse unites pardon, favor, and guidance. Success is not only external territory; it includes a purified relationship with Allah and steadfast direction. Believers celebrate victories best through seeking forgiveness, recognizing favor, and asking to remain straight rather than turning achievement into self-congratulation.\n\nThe sequence also honors the Messenger amid public negotiations. Opponents might interpret concession as weakness, while Allah announces forgiveness, completed favor, and straight guidance. Divine evaluation outweighs political appearance. The Prophet’s guidance along the Straight Path included choosing peace when peace served revelation and firmness when firmness was required. His example cannot be reduced to one tactic. For ordinary believers, asking forgiveness after success protects against hidden faults in intention and conduct. Guidance after success protects against drifting once pressure eases. Favor reaches completion when achievement returns the servant more gratefully to Allah.",
  "3": "“Allah will help you tremendously” completes the purposes connected to the clear triumph. The help is attributed directly to Allah and described as mighty, powerful, and worthy of honor. No opposing plan can prevent what He has decreed.\n\nAt Hudaybiyyah, help did not initially look like conquest. It appeared through restraint, negotiation, unity around the Prophet, and an agreement that opened future possibilities. Later developments made its strength visible. This teaches that divine help may work through calm judgment as well as dramatic victory.\n\nThe promise belongs especially to Muhammad in this verse. Believers benefit by following him, but they should not claim every personal project is guaranteed tremendous help. Divine support is sought through sincere faith, lawful means, patience, and obedience. Sometimes help protects the mission from the servants’ own rushed reactions.\n\n“Tremendously” also reassures the Prophet after painful years of rejection, battle, and exile. Allah’s support is not merely enough to survive; it can transform the political and spiritual landscape. Yet the Prophet responds with worship and humility. The greater the help, the greater the gratitude. A believer reading the verse learns not to equate gentleness with weakness, to trust Allah during unclear turns, and to recognize that the strongest help may be a wise opening whose consequences grow over time.\n\nThe Arabic description behind “tremendously” also conveys help that is mighty and unassailable. It encompasses protection from enemy plans and support that honors the one helped. But honor remains defined by Allah: at times it is the strength to accept terms that ego dislikes. The Companions would see concrete fruits, yet they first had to trust the promise. Communities can learn to evaluate support over a longer horizon, asking whether faith, safety, and access to truth have grown. They should still review decisions honestly, because trust in Allah is not an excuse to avoid planning or consultation.",
  "4": "“He is the One Who sent down serenity upon the hearts of the believers” describes Allah’s inward gift at Hudaybiyyah. The Companions faced disappointment, uncertainty, and terms they found painful, yet “serenity,” or “sakinah,” settled them so they could remain with the Prophet rather than be ruled by emotion.\n\n“So that they may increase even more in their faith” shows faith can increase. They already believed, then trust, obedience, and calm under a new test added to that faith. Sunni scholarship commonly uses this and similar verses as evidence that faith grows through obedience and can weaken through sin. The increase is Allah’s gift received through a faithful response.\n\n“To Allah alone belong the forces of the heavens and the earth.” He could have produced victory through armies, angels, wind, fear, or any created means. Choosing a treaty was not lack of power. Every force remained His, so believers could accept His chosen route.\n\n“Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” He knew their strained hearts, enemy plans, and future consequences. His wisdom placed serenity and selected the form of triumph. When outcomes confuse believers, these names provide an anchor: Allah never acts from ignorance or error. Serenity does not mean feeling nothing; it means a divinely strengthened heart that acts faithfully while feelings are intense. Prayer, remembrance, and obedience are means of welcoming that calm.\n\nThe “forces” include what humans can observe and what they cannot. A calm heart itself becomes one of Allah’s forces, capable of preventing panic from destroying a community. Numbers alone therefore do not explain victory. Knowledge and wisdom explain why aid arrives in a particular measure and moment. Believers seek serenity without claiming that anxiety proves lack of faith; even strong people feel disturbance before calm settles. They support one another, follow sound leadership, and remember that increase often occurs inside the very event they wished to escape. A test can become the setting where faith learns a deeper trust.",
  "5": "“So He may admit believing men and women into Gardens” states a purpose beyond political victory. Men and women are named explicitly, sharing faith, moral responsibility, and the promise of Paradise. Their roles and circumstances may vary, but neither sex is spiritually invisible before Allah.\n\n“Under which rivers flow” pictures continuous beauty, life, and provision. They enter “to stay there forever,” so safety is not interrupted by exile, treaty, death, or another conflict. Every worldly triumph is temporary; the supreme result is permanent admission.\n\n“And absolve them of their sins” places forgiveness beside reward. Paradise is entered through Allah’s mercy, while faith and obedience are real means He accepts. “Absolve” carries the sense of covering and removing the harmful consequence. Believers who struggled through Hudaybiyyah needed pardon as well as praise, and every believer needs it.\n\n“That is a supreme achievement in the sight of Allah.” His measure corrects human definitions of success. People may call territory, wealth, or reputation supreme, but Allah names forgiveness and eternal Gardens. The phrase “in the sight of Allah” is decisive because His evaluation lasts when public opinion changes.\n\nThe verse helps explain why the clear triumph matters: it serves faith and the road to final mercy. Worldly victories are valuable when they support that end. Believing men and women should therefore judge achievement by whether it leads toward forgiveness, steadfast worship, and the home where loss can never return.\n\nNaming women here also prevents a military-political reading of the surah from overlooking those whose faith, patience, families, and sacrifices sustained the community beyond the battlefield. Allah’s reward embraces all believers according to their sincere deeds. Gardens and absolution answer the two great needs of the servant: attainment of lasting good and removal of past evil. Either one without the other would leave the welcome incomplete. The verse calls this outcome “supreme” so every lesser objective remains in proportion. Success in career, family, scholarship, or public service is blessed when it helps rather than distracts from this achievement.",
  "6": "“He may punish hypocrite men and women and polytheistic men and women” balances the preceding promise to believing men and women. Both sexes are named because belief and accountability belong to each person. Hypocrites displayed faith while hiding rejection; polytheists openly directed worship beside Allah. Their outward positions differed, but both resisted the truth.\n\n“They harbour evil thoughts of Allah” identifies a root disease. At Hudaybiyyah they imagined Allah would abandon His Messenger and believers, or that Islam would be destroyed. A difficult moment became proof of failure in their minds because they judged Allah through hostility rather than His names and promises. An anxious thought that a believer dislikes is not the settled suspicion condemned here.\n\n“May ill-fate befall them!” means the evil outcome they expected for believers circles back upon them. “Allah is displeased with them” shows persistent hypocrisy and idolatry are not neutral before Him. “He has condemned them” means removal from His special mercy after chosen resistance.\n\n“Prepared for them Hell” gives the final consequence, and “What an evil destination!” asks listeners to judge every path by its end. The warning applies to those dying upon these states; living people can repent and become sincere. Its proper effect is not mistreatment of others, but urgency to purify one’s view of Allah. Good expectation rests on faith and obedience, not on demanding that every event immediately feel pleasant. Hudaybiyyah showed that divine wisdom can hide triumph inside apparent disappointment.\n\nEvil expectation can also shape conduct: once people assume Allah’s guidance will fail, betrayal begins to look sensible. The verse therefore links belief about the Lord with loyalty in action. A believer responds to uncertainty by recalling what Allah has revealed, asking for steadiness, and admitting limited sight. This does not mean claiming every personal wish will succeed. It means refusing to accuse Allah of injustice, weakness, or broken promise when His plan differs from one’s preferred timeline.",
  "7": "“To Allah alone belong the forces of the heavens and the earth” places every army, angel, wind, heart, natural power, and circumstance under His ownership. He needs no human support, though He honors servants by allowing them to serve His cause.\n\nThe statement answers hypocrisy and pride together. Those expecting Islam’s destruction forget that visible forces are a tiny part of creation. Believers trusting their own numbers make a related mistake. Means must be prepared, but no means works outside Allah’s decree. At Hudaybiyyah, restraint and serenity became forces of victory although no major battle occurred.\n\n“Allah is Almighty” means no one can overcome Him or prevent His purpose. His might secures those who obey and warns those secretly plotting. Yet might is joined to “All-Wise.” He uses power in the right manner, measure, and time. If He selects treaty rather than combat, delay rather than immediate entry, or an unseen opening rather than visible conquest, the choice is not weakness.\n\nTogether, these names protect balanced faith. Might without wisdom could leave a servant confused by delay; wisdom without might could be mistaken for merely good intention. Allah possesses both perfectly. Believers therefore work, consult, and stand firm without worshipping strategy. They refuse despair when events look unfavorable because the Lord of every force knows which one to deploy. His power never acts carelessly, and His wisdom never lacks the power to bring its purpose to completion.\n\nThe phrase about heavenly and earthly forces also widens attention beyond military power. Rain, sleep, timing, information, and a single changed decision can redirect history. Human beings often recognize only the final visible cause and forget the chain held by Allah. Gratitude names Him without denying the people or tools He used. Reliance likewise seeks those tools without assigning them independent control. This balance gives energy in action and peace after the outcome, because neither success nor delay lies outside the Almighty and All-Wise.",
  "8": "“We have sent you as a witness” describes Muhammad’s first role here. He witnesses that he conveyed revelation, and his life demonstrates how the message was practiced. Allah already knows everything; the Prophet’s testimony establishes public proof and honors his faithful service.\n\n“A deliverer of good news” makes hope central to his mission. He announces forgiveness, divine pleasure, support, and Paradise for those who believe and do good. This news is not flattery detached from conduct. It invites people toward faith and repentance through which Allah’s promise is received.\n\n“And a warner” completes the balance. Muhammad warned clearly against idolatry, hypocrisy, injustice, and Hell. Warning is mercy because announced danger can still be avoided. A messenger who said only what audiences enjoyed would not witness faithfully. Harshness that concealed good news would also distort his mission.\n\nThe verse teaches everyone who conveys Islam. Teachers should witness through truthful character, carry real hope, and explain consequences without invention. They cannot claim the Prophet’s unique authority, but they can follow his balance. Hudaybiyyah displayed all three roles: his obedience witnessed to Allah, the promised triumph brought good news, and hostile hypocrisy received warning. These roles prevent reducing Muhammad to a political leader. He was sent by Allah with a mission reaching conscience, society, death, and the final account. Loving him includes receiving the entire mission rather than selecting only its comforting or demanding side.\n\nA witness must communicate accurately, so the description also supports the reliability of Prophetic teaching. Muhammad did not conceal a difficult command or manufacture an easy promise. His verified Sunnah explains the Quran through word and conduct. Believers approach reports carefully, distinguishing authentic transmission from stories wrongly attributed to him. In doing so, they protect all three functions: false severity should not be presented as his warning, and unsupported promises should not be presented as his good news. Truthfulness honors the witness Allah sent.",
  "9": "“So that you believers may have faith in Allah and His Messenger” states the response to Muhammad’s mission. Faith in Allah includes worshipping Him alone and trusting His names, commands, and promises. Faith in the Messenger means accepting that Muhammad was truthfully sent and following his authentic teaching. Claimed devotion to Allah cannot knowingly reject the one He commissioned.\n\n“Support and honour him” refers to the Messenger. Support includes defending his message, helping its lawful work, and answering lies about him with truth. Honoring him means love, respect, careful speech, and obedience without raising him to divine status. The greatest honor is following what he taught, not inventing religion in his name.\n\n“Glorify Allah morning and evening” returns worship to Allah. Glorification declares Him free of every imperfection. Morning and evening name the edges of the day and imply regular remembrance throughout it. The prescribed prayers, daily praises, and obedient conduct prevent faith from becoming an occasional claim.\n\nRelationships are therefore ordered correctly: believe in Allah and His Messenger, support and respect the Messenger, and direct glorification to Allah. Love for Muhammad leads to the Lord he served; it never makes the servant a partner in worship. At Hudaybiyyah, believers honored him by accepting guidance when emotion resisted and glorified Allah for an opening they did not create. Daily remembrance helps later believers cultivate the same loyal and God-centered response in ordinary decisions.\n\nMorning and evening also suggest continuity across changing conditions. Believers glorify Allah in ease and strain, before an outcome becomes clear and after success appears. This guards support for the Messenger from becoming loyalty based only on worldly victory. He is honored because Allah sent him, not because circumstances always look favorable. Regular remembrance corrects the heart whenever public pressure, disappointment, or admiration threatens that order. Thus belief, support, honor, and glorification become a coherent daily way of life.",
  "10": "“Those who pledge allegiance to you O Prophet are actually pledging allegiance to Allah” refers especially to the pledge under the tree at Hudaybiyyah. The Companions promised steadfastness when danger appeared immediate. Obedience to Muhammad in this approved pledge was obedience to the One who sent him; it did not make Muhammad divine.\n\n“Allah’s Hand is over theirs” affirms His power, oversight, and support above the hands joined in pledge. Allah is unlike creation, so the expression is accepted without imagining a created form. It assures the pledgers that their covenant is witnessed and upheld by the Almighty.\n\n“Whoever breaks their pledge, it will only be to their own loss.” Betrayal cannot diminish Allah or defeat His Messenger. It destroys the betrayer’s integrity and reward. A covenant is not emotional speech limited to a powerful gathering; responsibility continues after the gathering ends.\n\n“Whoever fulfils their pledge to Allah, He will grant them a great reward.” Fulfillment requires patience, obedience, and truth when circumstances change. The reward exceeds the sacrifice. This event does not authorize modern individuals to invent unrestricted pledges or demand obedience in sin. Every human authority remains beneath Allah’s law, and creation is not obeyed through disobedience to the Creator. The lasting lesson is the seriousness of a lawful promise: Allah hears it, supports truthful fulfillment, and makes fidelity a road to reward. A believer should therefore promise carefully and honor the promise even after its immediate emotion has faded.\n\nThe pledge also reveals that religious commitment is ultimately made before Allah even when a human receives it. This intensifies honesty and limits the human receiver at once. The Prophet could receive the pledge because Allah authorized him; no later leader inherits unlimited sacred status. Lawful commitments to leaders, communities, marriages, employment, and public trusts still fall under the general duty to fulfill covenants. When an obligation conflicts with Allah’s command, divine obedience remains highest; when it is lawful, convenience is not a valid reason for betrayal.",
  "11": "“The nomadic Arabs, who stayed behind” were groups around Medina who did not accompany the Prophet to Hudaybiyyah. They expected danger and preferred safety, then prepared an acceptable-sounding explanation after the believers returned. Their nomadic identity was not the sin; revelation judges the hypocrisy and choice of particular people.\n\n“We were preoccupied with our wealth and families” turns normal responsibilities into an excuse. Wealth and family have real rights, and genuine inability can excuse a duty. Here the claim hid a darker calculation. “Ask for forgiveness for us” sounded repentant, but “They say with their tongues what is not in their hearts.” True repentance agrees inwardly, admits wrong, and changes direction.\n\n“Who then can stand between you and Allah in any way, if He intends harm or benefit for you?” exposes their faulty idea of safety. Remaining home cannot block decreed harm, and difficult obedience cannot prevent benefit Allah grants. Precaution is lawful, but a false family excuse does not control destiny.\n\n“Allah is All-Aware of what you do” reaches beyond words to motive and conduct. Communities should not dismiss every family-related absence as hypocrisy; people judge credible circumstances fairly. Yet polished language cannot deceive Allah. Family care becomes worship when sincere and rightly prioritized, but becomes a shield for cowardice when invoked falsely. Honest people state limitations truthfully, seek forgiveness for real failure, and trust that obedience never removes them from Allah’s care.\n\nThe request “ask for forgiveness for us” also teaches that religious language can be used to avoid the very repentance it names. Asking a righteous person to pray is not wrong, but prayer cannot substitute for a heart that intends to continue deception. The Prophet’s supplication did not give people control over Allah’s judgment. Each person must turn sincerely. When words and heart agree, asking others to pray can accompany repentance; when they disagree, the request becomes another layer of performance known fully to the All-Aware.",
  "12": "“You thought that the Messenger and the believers would never return to their families again” reveals what those staying behind concealed. Their absence was not mere busyness. They predicted the Muslim party would be destroyed, so joining appeared foolish. The safe return exposed their calculation.\n\n“That was made appealing in your hearts” shows how a fearful assumption became attractive. It justified self-protection and let them feel wiser than obedient believers. Sin often gains power by being decorated as realism, responsibility, or insight rather than appearing ugly. Satan and repeated desire beautify conclusions the heart already wants.\n\n“You harboured evil thoughts about Allah” identifies the deepest error. They treated His support as unreliable and His Messenger’s obedience as certain ruin. Believers may feel fear and assess risk, but they do not conclude that Allah abandons faithful obedience. Good expectation does not guarantee one imagined outcome; it trusts His wisdom and final promise.\n\n“You became a doomed people” means corrupt expectations and refusal placed them on a road to ruin. This warns without closing repentance to anyone alive. Their story teaches that hidden assumptions matter. A person should test discouraging forecasts against revelation, avoid calling cowardice prudence, and ask why a narrative feels appealing. Hudaybiyyah proved that the heart’s confident forecast can be exactly backward: those expecting death returned with serenity, divine pleasure, and the announcement of clear triumph.\n\nThe phrase “return to their families” also exposes the irony of their excuse. They claimed family concern kept them home, while privately believing the Prophet’s companions would lose their own families forever. True care is not selfish exemption from every shared duty. Families thrive when members trust Allah, fulfill communal obligations according to capacity, and refuse to build security upon others’ sacrifice alone. The verse does not diminish family responsibility; it corrects the use of family as a respectable cover for disbelief in Allah’s care.",
  "13": "“Whoever does not believe in Allah and His Messenger” states the essential issue after excuses are exposed. Faith cannot accept Allah while knowingly rejecting the Messenger He sent. Muhammad’s message is the authorized path by which this community learns worship, guidance, and final warning.\n\nThe general “whoever” crosses tribe, sex, wealth, and location. Nomadic background does not condemn, and closeness to Medina does not save. Each person answers for the response to clear guidance. Sincere uncertainty should receive patient evidence; the verse concerns real nonbelief, whose inward extent Allah knows perfectly.\n\n“We surely have prepared for the disbelievers a blazing Fire” confirms that disbelief carried into death has eternal consequence. “Prepared” gives certainty rather than a threat that might be forgotten. “Blazing” communicates intense punishment and urgently calls listeners away. The warning comes from the Lord who just promised Gardens and forgiveness to believing men and women.\n\nNo believer may use this verse to assume a named living person’s final destination or commit injustice against non-Muslims. Worldly dealings remain governed by justice, covenants, and wise invitation. Final judgment belongs to Allah, who knows what message reached each person and how it was answered. For the reader, safety lies in renewing faith in both Allah and His Messenger and refusing to hide rejection beneath acceptable excuses. The Fire is mentioned while escape through sincere faith and repentance remains possible.\n\nBelief in the Messenger includes accepting the Quran he delivered, his final prophethood, and his established explanation of worship. It is more than acknowledging that a historical teacher existed. At the same time, this belief is never worship of Muhammad; he remained Allah’s servant and messenger. The two objects of faith in the opening phrase establish this distinction. Allah is believed in as Lord and God, while Muhammad is believed in as the truthful human envoy whose guidance must be followed because the Lord authorized it.",
  "14": "“To Allah alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth” answers anyone imagining safety, punishment, or reward outside His control. His kingdom includes visible authorities and unseen realities. He owns by creation and sovereign right, while human ownership is temporary, limited, and accountable.\n\n“He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills” places final judgment with Him, exercised through perfect justice and knowledge. His will is not arbitrary. Revelation explains that sincere repentance is accepted and that dying upon unrepented association with Him is not forgiven. Countless individual details are known only to Him.\n\n“Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” ends with hope after warning. The order invites those who stayed behind and lied to repent instead of despairing. “All-Forgiving” indicates vast and repeated forgiveness; “Most Merciful” means mercy belongs perfectly to Him. Punishment is deserved justice, while forgiveness is generous grace.\n\nThe verse prevents opposite errors. No person can guarantee forgiveness while defiantly persisting, and no person can close a door Allah leaves open. His ownership means no institution or imagined intercessor can overrule Him, but His names encourage return. Truthful confession, corrected faith, and repaired conduct are better than another excuse. Believers remain humble because hope does not rest on controlling the verdict. It rests on serving the King whose mercy exceeds their sins and whose judgment cannot be manipulated.\n\nFor those addressed after Hudaybiyyah, the names were a direct invitation. Their secret fear and lies were serious, but they had not yet died upon them. The King who could punish also described Himself as forgiving and merciful. A person should never use divine decree as an excuse to remain unchanged, saying forgiveness belongs only to whomever Allah wills. Revelation shows that willing repentance is itself a road He loves and commands. The servant moves toward mercy while leaving the final judgment humbly with its Owner.",
  "15": "“Those who stayed behind will say, when you believers set out to take the spoils of war, ‘Let us accompany you.’” Danger has passed, and gain now makes participation attractive. This change is not repentance; they want the reward of obedience they refused when obedience looked costly.\n\n“They wish to change Allah’s promise” refers to gains reserved for those who accompanied the Prophet at Hudaybiyyah, especially in relation to Khaybar. Allowing opportunists to join would alter the announced allocation. “You will not accompany us. This is what Allah has said before” makes exclusion revelation, not personal revenge.\n\n“You are driven by jealousy against us!” is their counteraccusation. Instead of examining earlier distrust, they reinterpret obedience as envy. A resistant heart often assigns an ugly motive to a person enforcing a rightful boundary. “They can hardly comprehend” means they grasp little of Allah’s wisdom and the moral cause of the decision.\n\nThe verse distinguishes forgiveness from entitlement. A repentant person can return to Allah without demanding every missed worldly opportunity. Consequences may remain after forgiveness, and rewards tied to earlier sacrifice need not pass to those who avoided it. Leaders cannot use this passage for favoritism; this restriction came from Allah. The broader lesson is to serve before profit appears, receive correction without invented accusations, and accept that sincere return seeks Allah’s pleasure rather than immediate access to benefits.\n\nTheir accusation of jealousy also illustrates projection. They themselves were moved by desire for wealth, then imagined the believers’ refusal must also arise from a worldly passion. Revelation supplies the missing moral category: people may deny a profitable request simply because Allah said no. Understanding faith requires believing that obedience can overrule material interest. When this category is absent, sincerity is continually translated into rivalry. The remedy is learning Allah’s promise, acknowledging one’s prior choice, and allowing moral truth rather than wounded pride to explain the boundary.",
  "16": "“You will be called to fight against a people of great might” gives those who stayed behind another future test. Exclusion from immediate spoils is not permanent exclusion from obedience. They will face a formidable opponent, allowing their claim to be proved where sacrifice is again real. Commentators differed over the historical people intended, but the moral test is clear without insisting upon one identification.\n\n“You will fight unless they submit” concerns a lawful campaign under legitimate Muslim authority, never private violence. Submission here is interpreted within historical and legal context. The statement cannot be detached from Quranic rules governing treaties, noncombatants, and justice.\n\n“If you then obey, Allah will grant you a fine reward.” Past failure does not remove every road to good. Obedience in the next test can demonstrate change and bring reward beyond worldly gains. “If you turn away as you did before,” repeated refusal exposes the excuse and brings “a painful punishment.”\n\nAccountability is therefore joined with another opportunity. The verse neither pretends the first betrayal was harmless nor defines people forever by it. Genuine repentance accepts the next duty even when easy profit is absent. Modern readers leave armed conflict to lawful authority and qualified judgment. The personal principle remains: when a new opportunity to obey follows an old failure, meet it honestly rather than repeating the pattern that words once concealed.\n\n“A fine reward” is deliberately broader than spoils. If they obey sincerely, the most important gain is Allah’s acceptance, whether or not worldly provision accompanies it. This corrects the motive exposed in the preceding verse. They asked to join when spoils were near; they are now called toward a duty where struggle is near. The contrast gives repentance a practical shape. Real change serves when the outcome is uncertain, whereas opportunism appears only when profit can already be counted. Allah knows which response comes from the heart.",
  "17": "“There is no blame on the blind, or the disabled, or the sick for staying behind” establishes clear exemptions from military participation. Islam does not measure faith by forcing people into duties beyond physical capacity. Blindness, mobility limitation, and illness can make combat impossible or seriously harmful, so Allah removes blame.\n\nThe exemption is legal and compassionate, not a claim that disabled people lack value. They may excel in knowledge, counsel, worship, work, generosity, and other service. Individuals differ, so ability for one contribution need not remove exemption from another. Communities should provide dignity and access rather than discussing disabled people only through absence.\n\n“Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger will be admitted by Him into Gardens under which rivers flow.” Reward is opened to everyone through obedience suited to their circumstances. Paradise is not reserved for physical strength. Allah admits the servant, and flowing rivers express lasting life and provision.\n\n“Whoever turns away will be subjected by Him to a painful punishment.” Turning away is deliberate refusal, not inability Allah explicitly excused. This protects vulnerable people from shame while preserving responsibility for the able. No leader should manipulate illness into guilt, and no able person should imitate disability as an excuse. Allah knows capacity perfectly. The verse teaches compassionate law: duties are real, exemptions are real, and both belong to obedience. A faithful community enables each member to serve within ability and never confuses bodily limitation with spiritual failure.\n\nThe verse also challenges communities to distinguish exemption from exclusion. Someone excused from battle remains fully part of the believers and shares the central invitation to Allah and His Messenger. The early Muslim community included disabled Companions whose contributions and dignity were recognized. Accessibility today follows the same moral direction: teaching, mosques, communication, and service should be arranged so bodily differences do not create needless barriers. Mercy in law should become mercy in communal design, while personal obedience flourishes through whatever sound abilities Allah has given.",
  "18": "“Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you O Prophet under the tree” announces a great honor for the Companions at Hudaybiyyah. Amid a report that Uthman might have been killed in Mecca, they pledged steadfastness and loyalty. This became the Pledge of Ridwan, named for the divine pleasure declared here.\n\n“He knew what was in their hearts” explains that Allah’s approval rested on more than appearance. He knew sincerity, faith, courage, and human strain. Muslims honor the people of this pledge because Allah testified to their inward condition at that moment. Claims broadly portraying them as secret traitors conflict with this praise.\n\n“He sent down serenity upon them” shows courage as gift and choice. The tense situation could have created panic or division, but Allah settled their hearts. Serenity does not erase emotion; it orders emotion beneath trust and obedience.\n\n“Rewarded them with a victory at hand” points to near success, often connected with Khaybar and gains after Hudaybiyyah, while the treaty itself was already called a clear triumph. Willingness to sacrifice came before visible reward. Allah sees sincere resolve before results appear. Later believers cannot claim His pleasure for themselves with certainty, but they can seek its causes: truthful covenant, loyalty to Prophetic guidance, courage within lawful limits, and hearts receptive to serenity from their Lord.\n\nThe historical tree itself is not the source of blessing independently of Allah. The Quran honors the believers, their pledge, and the Lord’s pleasure, not an object to be worshipped. Umar later acted to prevent unhealthy attachment when people sought the location. Love for sacred history remains sound when it leads to imitation of faith rather than superstition. The enduring memorial is the revealed verse: Allah knew sincere hearts, sent tranquility, and rewarded costly loyalty with an opening that those hearts could not yet fully see.",
  "19": "“Many spoils of war they will gain” continues the near reward promised to the believers of the pledge. Material gain did not create their sincerity; they pledged when danger, not wealth, stood before them. Because they proved faithful without visible profit, Allah later granted lawful provision.\n\nCommentators connect these spoils especially with Khaybar and later openings. Islamic law regulates such gains through authority and distribution; this is no permission for individuals to seize property or call theft a religious prize. What became lawful did so through revelation and rules governing a recognized campaign.\n\n“Allah is Almighty” assures that fortresses and opposing forces cannot prevent provision He decrees. “All-Wise” means gains come with right timing and allocation. He withheld immediate entry into Mecca yet opened another provision, teaching that denied expectations do not equal denied care.\n\nThe short verse also tests motives after success. Believers receive wealth while remembering serenity, pledge, and obedience came first. Spoils should create gratitude, not attachment that corrupts the cause. Modern Muslims read this as established sacred history, not a program for unauthorized war. Its broader lesson is that Allah may reward sacrifice materially and spiritually, lawful wealth is not shameful, and every gain remains subject to His commands. Provision is safest when received as entrusted reward rather than proof of superiority.\n\nBecause the gains were “many,” the promise also displays abundance after a season of apparent restriction. The pilgrims had been blocked from Mecca and returned without completing the intended pilgrimage, but their Lord was not withholding all good. One closed route did not empty His provision. This helps believers avoid interpreting a particular denial as total abandonment. They may grieve the closed door while remaining alert to lawful doors opening elsewhere. Wisdom lies in receiving the new provision gratefully without pretending the original disappointment was unreal.",
  "20": "“Allah has promised you believers abundant spoils, which you will gain” widens the horizon beyond one event. The community would receive further lawful gains as opposition receded. The promise came while circumstances looked uncertain, teaching reliance on Allah’s knowledge of the future.\n\n“He hastened this truce for you” identifies Hudaybiyyah as an immediate gift. Though some Companions found its terms painful, peace protected lives, reduced hostility, and opened space for the message. The translation makes the near gift the truce rather than only material spoils.\n\n“He has held people’s hands back from harming you” includes protection from enemies who might attack the pilgrims or threaten Medina in their absence. Restraint can be as real a victory as defeating an attacker. Allah controls hands by known and unseen means, while believers still guard responsibly.\n\n“So it may be a sign for the believers” means fulfilled protection strengthens faith in later promises. “So He may guide you along the Straight Path” shows events can educate. The straight path includes trusting Prophetic judgment, recognizing peace as help, and using gains without losing moral direction. Success is not merely receiving resources; it is remaining guided through them. The verse trains believers to notice prevented harms, not only visible gifts. A danger that never reached them may be one of Allah’s greatest favors, although it leaves no dramatic story.\n\nGuidance “along the Straight Path” after protection prevents a common error: people may seek Allah desperately during danger and then wander once safe. The sign is meant to deepen obedience, not merely provide relief. The treaty created new possibilities for teaching, travel, and interaction; those possibilities needed moral direction. Every protected opportunity raises a question about its use. If peace only produces heedlessness, its lesson is missed. If it produces clearer faith, wider invitation, and grateful service, the worldly protection has become guidance toward eternal good.",
  "21": "“There are other gains which are beyond your reach” tells believers that present ability does not define future possibility. Victories or benefits lay outside what they could then imagine or achieve. Human planning sees current resources; revelation announces that Allah’s store is larger.\n\n“Allah is keeping in store for you” means He has encompassed and reserved those gains through knowledge and decree. What servants cannot reach is easy for their Lord. Commentators connected this with later conquests and differed over specific territories. The wording can embrace broad openings without forcing only one identification.\n\n“Allah is Most Capable of everything” supplies the foundation. His power does not depend upon present numbers, equipment, or political reach. Yet promise did not cancel preparation. The Prophet continued organizing, negotiating, teaching, and acting; divine ability worked through obedient means.\n\nThis offers hope without authorizing wishful claims about every project. This was revelation to Muhammad and his community in a defined setting. Believers today know Allah can open what seems closed, but do not know that a particular worldly gain has been promised. They ask, plan lawfully, and accept His choice. The most important stored gains may be rewards no eye presently sees. Awareness of complete divine ability prevents despair, while humility about His specific decree prevents presumption. Faith works between those two protections.\n\nBeing “beyond your reach” can describe a present fact without defining permanent destiny. This language encourages realistic assessment: the believers truly could not obtain those gains then. Faith did not require pretending otherwise. Hope entered by remembering whose power surrounds the limitation. A person may therefore say, “I cannot currently reach this,” without saying, “Allah can never open it.” Such speech joins honesty about weakness with confidence in divine ability. It always supports careful effort and protects against both fantasy and despair.",
  "22": "“If the disbelievers were to fight you” describes conflict that might have erupted around Hudaybiyyah. The believers appeared vulnerable as pilgrims near hostile Mecca, but appearance did not decide the result. Had opponents begun battle there, Allah declares believers would have been supported.\n\n“They would certainly flee” foretells retreat for the aggressors. This is a specific assurance, not a claim that every Muslim army wins regardless of justice, preparation, leadership, or obedience. Elsewhere the Quran records setbacks and their causes. Here Allah reveals what would have happened in this encounter.\n\n“They would never find any protector or helper” means no ultimate ally could shield them from His decree. A protector could attempt defense and a helper offer aid, but none could overcome Allah. Their idols had no power, while political alliances remained created and limited.\n\nThis helps explain why accepting truce was not cowardice. Muhammad chose peace while possessing divine support; restraint came from wisdom, not helplessness. Confidence in Allah does not require seeking combat, and peacemaking does not prove fear. If fighting had been imposed, believers were to stand; when treaty served guidance, they accepted Prophetic judgment. Strength under control is more faithful than aggression used to prove courage. The Lord who can make an enemy flee can also grant victory by holding every hand back.\n\nThe mention of protector and helper echoes the surah’s emphasis that all forces belong to Allah. An enemy may count alliances while overlooking the Lord who governs every ally. Believers should not copy this error from the opposite side by treating political support as a guarantee. They fulfill agreements and value assistance, but the heart depends finally on Allah. If He grants peace, they are grateful; if defense becomes unavoidable, they seek His help through lawful preparation. In both cases, confidence remains disciplined by obedience rather than turned into boastfulness.",
  "23": "“Allah’s way, already long established in the past” places promised support within a repeated divine pattern. Across prophetic history, persistent aggression against clear truth may dominate temporarily but cannot defeat Allah’s purpose. Messengers and sincere followers receive final vindication in the form His wisdom selects.\n\nA divine “way” does not mean every event is identical. Prophets faced varied tests: some communities were destroyed, some believers migrated, and some victories came through patience or treaty. The stable principle is that Allah’s decree and moral order cannot be overturned by hostile power.\n\n“You will find no change in Allah’s way” gives certainty. People cannot pressure Him into abandoning justice or make falsehood permanently victorious over His revealed purpose. This does not let readers predict exact dates, identify every political cause as prophetic truth, or ignore conditions placed on support. Human claims can be mistaken while Allah’s way never is.\n\nHudaybiyyah broadened the believers’ understanding of that way. They might expect support only as immediate entry or battlefield victory, but Allah provided truce, protection, and future opening. The verse creates firmness with intellectual humility. Trust the unchanging Lord, but remain teachable about changing means He selects. Study earlier revelation for patterns, obey within present circumstances, and never turn one preferred strategy into part of what Allah promised never to change.\n\nAn unchanging way also means moral cause and consequence are not random, even when the interval is long. Patience is necessary because people often see only a small segment of the pattern. Earlier messengers endured years before the promised outcome, and some faithful servants died before worldly vindication appeared. Their final vindication is not lost, because the Hereafter completes Allah’s justice. Therefore “no change” should be read across the full horizon revelation provides, not reduced to a guarantee of immediate comfort for every righteous individual.",
  "24": "“He is the One Who held back their hands from you and your hands from them” attributes mutual restraint at Hudaybiyyah to Allah. Reports mention hostile groups attempting action and coming under Muslim control, yet the encounter did not become a broad bloodbath. Allah protected believers while preventing them from striking in a way that would obstruct the wiser opening.\n\n“In the valley of Hudaibiyah, near Mecca” grounds the sign in a known place. Revelation explains actual history, not merely a general sense of peace. The location was tense because Muslims approached the sacred city and Quraysh blocked them.\n\n“After giving you the upper hand over a group of them” shows restraint followed ability. Believers were not simply unable to respond; Allah gave control and guided its use. Disciplined release carries greater moral meaning when retaliation was possible.\n\n“Allah is All-Seeing of what you do” includes enemy action, Muslim restraint, intentions, and every hidden risk. His sight supports accurate judgment where participants see only part. Prevented violence can be a deliberate divine victory. Communities should not measure leadership only by enemies struck; lives protected and conflicts contained matter too. This does not excuse neglecting defense of the vulnerable. It honors controlled strength under Prophetic guidance when justice, safety, and future guidance are served better by hands being held back than by bloodshed.\n\nMutual restraint further shows that Allah may protect both sides from actions whose consequences neither fully grasps. Some among the opponents would later accept Islam, and bloodshed could have hardened barriers. Believers do not know the future identity of an enemy, so they follow present law and leave hearts to Allah. When a just, secure resolution prevents unnecessary killing, gratitude is appropriate rather than embarrassment that no dramatic conquest occurred. The All-Seeing Lord can make disciplined non-action serve truth more powerfully than an impulsive strike.",
  "25": "“They are the ones who persisted in disbelief and hindered you from the Sacred Mosque” names Quraysh’s wrong although a treaty was reached. Peace did not turn injustice into innocence. They blocked believers from worship and prevented “the sacrificial animals from reaching their destination,” interfering with peaceful sacred rites.\n\nAllah explains why immediate battle was withheld: “Had there not been believing men and women, unknown to you.” Some Muslims concealed faith within Mecca. The approaching believers could not identify them, and fighting might have caused them to be “trampled underfoot,” bringing guilt and grief through unintended harm.\n\n“That was so Allah may admit into His mercy whoever He wills.” Restraint protected hidden believers and allowed others time to embrace faith. Divine mercy considered people unseen by the army and changes no commander could predict. “Had those unknown believers stood apart,” persistent disbelievers would have faced painful punishment. Their presence delayed consequence.\n\nA just cause therefore does not make unidentified innocent harm irrelevant. When believers could not be distinguished, even deserved punishment upon aggressors was withheld. Modern applications require qualified law and careful facts, but this principle opposes reckless collective destruction. Allah knew every hidden heart; people did not. Incomplete intelligence should increase precaution. Hudaybiyyah’s restraint protected worshippers, opened mercy, and prevented believers from carrying blood they never intended to shed. Protection of unseen innocents was itself part of the clear triumph.\n\nThe hidden believers also remind readers not to classify a whole city as one moral block. Mecca contained persecutors, uncertain seekers, and faithful people unable to declare themselves. Collective labels can conceal precisely those whom justice must protect. Allah’s knowledge separated every person even when human sight could not. The safe response to uncertainty was restraint, not assuming innocent loss was acceptable. This verse gives strategic wisdom a deeply spiritual foundation: caution matters because every unknown life is fully known to Allah and may already belong to His believing servants.",
  "26": "“The disbelievers had filled their hearts with pride” describes Quraysh at Hudaybiyyah. Their pride was not healthy dignity but stubborn self-importance that blocked pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque. “The pride of pre-Islamic ignorance” identifies a tribal mentality that defended status and custom even against truth and sacred duty.\n\n“Allah sent down His serenity upon His Messenger and the believers” presents the opposite inner condition. The disbelievers were heated by pride; believers were settled by divine calm. Serenity enabled them to accept treaty terms, control anger, and obey Muhammad when retaliation might have felt more honorable. True strength was mastery under guidance.\n\n“Inspiring them to uphold the declaration of faith” refers to the word of piety, commonly explained as the testimony that none is worthy of worship except Allah. They remained bound to monotheism and its moral demands. “They were better entitled and more worthy of it” because sincere belief had prepared their hearts to carry that declaration faithfully.\n\n“Allah has perfect knowledge of all things” means He knew which side held sincere faith, what pride concealed, and why restraint served the future. People could see treaty wording; He knew every consequence. The verse asks believers to identify ignorance not merely with lack of information but with proud conduct that rejects divine limits. Knowledge should produce serenity, principled restraint, and fidelity to faith rather than the heated need to win every appearance.\n\nThe “declaration of faith” also restrained tribal ego because it places all greatness with Allah. Quraysh defended prestige by refusing wording that acknowledged Muhammad as Messenger in the treaty document, while believers knew his status did not depend on an opponent writing it. They could accept removal of a title from that document without removing faith from their hearts. Serenity separated essential truth from symbolic provocation. Mature conviction does not need to turn every insult into an uncontrolled reaction; it remains loyal while choosing the response most pleasing to Allah.",
  "27": "“Allah will fulfil His Messenger’s vision in all truth” refers to Muhammad’s true dream that he and the believers would enter the Sacred Mosque. Prophetic dreams are revelation. The vision promised entry but did not state it must occur during the first journey, so the Hudaybiyyah delay did not make it false.\n\n“Allah willing, you will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, in security” combines confident promise with acknowledgment that everything occurs by Allah’s will. The believers would enter without the fear surrounding their first attempt. “Some with heads shaved and others with hair shortened” describes completion of pilgrimage rites; shaving and shortening mark release from the pilgrim state.\n\n“He knew what you did not know” explains the gap between promise and timing. Allah knew hidden believers in Mecca, benefits of truce, future conversions, and the safest route to fulfillment. Human disappointment came from seeing only the immediate journey. “He first granted you the triumph at hand” means another victory arrived before the envisioned entry.\n\nThe verse teaches precision in trusting revelation. Allah fulfills what He promises, but servants must not insert a timetable He did not give. “Allah willing” is not doubt in His specific promise; it teaches submission and proper speech about future action. Believers plan confidently while leaving timing and route to perfect knowledge. Delay can preserve the promise and prepare a fuller fulfillment.\n\nThe details of shaved and shortened hair make the vision concrete. It was not a vague promise later reinterpreted to fit anything; the believers would perform recognizable rites in safety, and they did. Yet concrete fulfillment still came through an unexpected sequence. This protects faith from two errors: abandoning a true promise because it was not immediate, and making promises so vague that nothing can test them. Allah’s knowledge held both precision and timing. The servant’s knowledge held only the revealed destination, so patient obedience bridged the distance.",
  "28": "“He is the One Who has sent His Messenger with right guidance” attributes Muhammad’s mission to Allah. Guidance includes true knowledge, sound worship, and the path that brings people from confusion toward their Lord. Muhammad did not design a private spiritual program; he carried revelation authorized by the Creator.\n\n“And the religion of truth” describes Islam as true in belief, law, worship, and final purpose. Its truth is not produced by political success. Rather, its promised prevalence follows from its divine source. “Making it prevail over all others” has been explained as Allah making its proofs and truth superior and granting historical ascendancy, with complete manifestation according to His promise.\n\n“And sufficient is Allah as a Witness” removes dependence on hostile recognition. Allah witnesses that He sent Muhammad and that the message is true. His testimony appears through revelation, signs, fulfilled promises, and the Quran’s enduring proof. No human denial can cancel it.\n\nPrevalence does not license forced conversion or injustice. Faith cannot be made sincere through compulsion, and Islamic dominance remains bound to Prophetic mercy, covenants, and justice. The verse came amid a treaty, showing that truth can advance through peaceful contact as well as political opening. Believers support its prevalence first by learning and living it accurately, not by attaching its name to conduct that contradicts its guidance.\n\n“The religion of truth” also sets a standard for those representing it. Lies, oppression, and superstition do not become true because a Muslim employs them. The religion prevails by its own divine evidence, while its followers are honored when their conduct reflects that evidence. If they fail, their failure does not make revelation false, but it may hide its beauty from others and bring accountability. Confidence in Allah’s witness should therefore create integrity, not laziness. He witnesses both the message’s truth and what its professed followers do with it.",
  "29": "“Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah” states his identity and authority plainly. He is neither divine nor merely an admired reformer. “Those with him are firm with the disbelievers and compassionate with one another.” Firmness means principled strength against hostile rejection, not cruelty toward every non-Muslim. Compassion governs believers’ mutual relationships and should appear in support, forgiveness, and sincere counsel.\n\n“You see them bowing and prostrating in prayer, seeking Allah’s bounty and pleasure.” Their public strength grows from humble worship. They seek provision and grace, but above all divine acceptance. “The sign of brightness can be seen on their faces from the trace of prostrating” refers to the light, humility, and effect of worship, not necessarily a physical mark that proves righteousness.\n\n“This is their description in the Torah.” Their qualities were known in earlier revelation. The Gospel parable compares them to “a seed that sprouts its tiny branches,” then grows strong, thick, and firm, delighting planters. The small persecuted community developed under Prophetic cultivation into a stable people.\n\nAllah thereby makes believers “a source of dismay for the disbelievers” who hate guidance, because healthy growth frustrates their wish for its disappearance. This is not an invitation to provoke people through bad character. “To those of them who believe and do good,” Allah promises forgiveness and great reward. The ending prevents relying on mere companionship or label; saving promise belongs to persevering faith and good action.\n\nThe seed image also teaches gradual development. A branch begins tiny, receives nourishment, strengthens alongside others, and eventually helps the whole planting stand. New believers and young communities need cultivation rather than contempt for being small. Growth becomes delightful when rooted, balanced, and mutually supporting. Firmness without compassion makes the plant harsh; compassion without principle leaves it unable to stand. Bowing and prostration nourish both qualities. The final promise directs admiration away from personality worship and toward the faith and good deeds Allah accepts."
};
